async function loadUser() {
  const data = encodeURIComponent(JSON.stringify({ id: 7, name: "Mina" }));
  const response = await fetch("data:application/json," + data);

  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
  const user = await response.json();
  console.log(user.name, response.bodyUsed);
}

loadUser();

async function readJson(response) {
  const text = await response.text();
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${text}`);
  }
  return text ? JSON.parse(text) : null;
}

const response = new Response("missing user", { status: 404 });
readJson(response).catch(error => console.log(error.message));

sections.content:
**Failure layers.** A request can reach different boundaries successfully. Transport can produce an HTTP envelope even when the application reports failure. Body decoding and schema validation happen later and can fail independently.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  F[await fetch] -->|reject| N[Network, scheme, or abort failure]
  F -->|Response| O{response.ok?}
  O -->|No| H[Handle HTTP status and error body]
  O -->|Yes| P[Decode expected body]
  P -->|parse fails| D[Decoding failure]
  P -->|valid parse| V[Validate application shape]
```

**Runnable example.** A locally created 404 Response demonstrates that HTTP failure and Promise rejection are different outcomes.

```javascript
async function readJson(response) {
  if (!response.ok) {
    const message = await response.text();
    throw new Error("HTTP " + response.status + ": " + message);
  }
  return response.json();
}

const response = new Response("missing user", { status: 404 });
try { await readJson(response); } catch (error) {
  console.log(error.message); // HTTP 404: missing user
}
```

**Boundary.** A status policy belongs to the API contract. Redirects, 304, and 204 have legitimate meanings, so a shared helper should not assume every non-JSON or non-200 response is identical.

