async function fetchWithTimeout(url, ms) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), ms);

  try {
    return await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timer);
  }
}

sections.content:
**Signal flow.** The controller owns the action; the signal carries notification to consumers. A timeout, navigation cleanup, and “cancel” button can all call the same abort boundary without giving the request function knowledge of UI details.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  C[AbortController] --> S[signal passed to operation]
  T[timeout or UI cleanup] -->|abort| C
  S --> O[fetch or other abort-aware work]
  O -->|abort observed| R[rejected Promise]
  R --> H[ignore, report timeout, or retry by policy]
```

**Runnable example.** This local abort-aware operation uses the same signal contract as Fetch without depending on a network server.

```javascript
function wait(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => resolve("done"), ms);
    signal.addEventListener("abort", () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException("Aborted", "AbortError"));
    }, { once: true });
  });
}

const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(new Error("timed out")), 5);
try { await wait(50, controller.signal); } catch (error) {
  console.log(error.message); // timed out
}
```

**Boundary.** A reusable operation should also check `signal.aborted` before registering its listener; the short example starts with a fresh signal, while production helpers must handle signals already aborted by their caller.
